{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/service at exact clean pushed head a84ad547d5893b664ff537b1d84b778dcaa29a07, branch request/resident-browser-security-headers. Fulfils request f26cf90c under promise 44697072.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. A new internal/service/headers.go carrying one map of four response headers and one wrapper that writes them, and one line in server.go putting that wrapper outside the existing observability wrapper. Values: Content-Security-Policy frame-ancestors 'none', X-Frame-Options DENY, X-Content-Type-Options nosniff, Referrer-Policy no-referrer -- the strict end of each range, which suits a surface with exactly one legitimate viewer.\n\nTHE PLACEMENT IS THE DESIGN, AND IT IS NOT ARBITRARY. observe.HTTPHandler returns its argument unchanged when there is no observer. A policy installed inside it would therefore vanish whenever observability was off, silently, in exactly the deployment least likely to be watched. The wrapper goes outside it instead. That also makes the request's hardest condition structural rather than merely tested: headers written before any handler runs cannot be dropped by a handler, including on an early return, because the header map is what reaches the client when WriteHeader fires.\n\nI VERIFIED THE AUDIT BEFORE STARTING. This request refreshes a stale self-request, so the premise deserved checking rather than inheriting. On the base, grepping internal/service and ui for all five header names returned nothing: no response carried any protection. The finding was real and current.\n\nTESTS, AND WHAT THEY ARE FOR. Five representative routes: the UI document, an embedded asset, an API success, an API error, and an unknown path. The unknown path is deliberate -- a 404 is written by net/http rather than by our code, and is exactly what a per-handler policy would miss. The asset path is read from the committed embed at test time so the test cannot drift into requesting a filename that no longer ships. A separate test pins the four values, because \"frame-ancestors 'self'\" would pass a presence-only check while leaving the risk open. A third drives the real error path, a malformed POST to /v0/act, rather than a stand-in.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE. Three mutations, each caught by both route tests: removing the secureHeaders wrapper; weakening frame-ancestors from 'none' to 'self'; deleting nosniff from the map. Green here is therefore load-bearing rather than incidental.\n\nLIVE EVIDENCE, BECAUSE nosniff CAN BREAK A PAGE. Unit tests cannot tell whether the real server sends types the browser will accept once sniffing is forbidden, so I ran a resident and read the wire. GET / returns all four headers with Content-Type text/html; charset=utf-8. GET /assets/index-kKWJk2SQ.js returns the headers with Content-Type text/javascript; charset=utf-8, which is the specific thing nosniff would otherwise break. A 404 carries the headers too. The shipped embed survives this policy.\n\nWHAT I DELIBERATELY DID NOT DO. No full document CSP. frame-ancestors answers the framing risk and can be asserted without a browser; script-src and style-src for the embedded UI cannot be, and a policy that silently broke the shipped page would be worse than the gap it closed. That is a bounded omission, stated in the code, not an oversight.\n\nGATES at the exact head, all run: go test -count=1 ./internal/service 17.386s and ./internal/wireparity 0.574s; go test -count=1 ./... with no failing package; go vet ./... clean; gofmt clean; make docs ok 46.082s; git diff --check clean; worktree clean and pushed. The embed is untouched, so make ui-check has nothing to regenerate.","body":{"branch":"request/resident-browser-security-headers","commit":"a84ad547d5893b664ff537b1d84b778dcaa29a07","path":"internal/service","tests":"go test -count=1 ./internal/service ./internal/wireparity; go test -count=1 ./...; go vet ./...; make docs; git diff --check; live header check against a running resident"}}